./ralph/progress.txt:32917:- Implemented: internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_repository_invariants_test.go — repository-layer CRUD-and-lifecycle invariants for the quota_usage table. quota_usage is the per-tenant counter table; its ONLY public write surface in QuotaRepository is LockUsage, which INSERTs the counter row ON CONFLICT (organization_id, resource) DO NOTHING and then SELECTs FOR UPDATE. There is no per-row Update/Delete method, no version column, and no service_id pointer — the increment path is owned by quota.Checker and the delete-cascade is owned by schema CASCADE. The 9-test file pins what neither quota_repository_test.go (the original LockUsage happy-path test) nor the schema/tenant-isolation siblings already cover: (1) row-shape on the INSERT leg — qusg_ prefix, used_value defaults to 0, created_at/updated_at within 1s; (2) second-call byte-identity — id, created_at, updated_at, AND used_value all anchored across ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING (the set_updated_at trigger fires BEFORE UPDATE only; a non-UPDATE must not stamp updated_at — proved with a measurable wallclock gap between calls); (3) peer-row byte-identity — LockUsage(org, services) does not touch the (org, projects) counter row's id/created_at/updated_at/used_value, and the two rows have distinct ids; (4) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING discards the minted qusg_ id when the row exists — proved by pre-seeding a row at used_value=17 with id `qu_seed_projects` and observing both the live used_value and the original id survive a LockUsage call; (5) FK violation (unknown organization) -> typed Conflict via mapWriteError; (6) DOMAIN violation by casting `"not-a-resource"` to store.QuotaResource -> typed Conflict; (7) transaction rollback — closure returns sentinel after a successful LockUsage, Write rolls back, no quota_usage row persists (counter probe = 0); (8) nil-tx -> yerr.CodeInternal. The 9th test is the pre-existing TestQuotaRepositoryLockUsageCreatesAndReturnsCounter that the file does NOT duplicate — it lives in quota_repository_test.go and remains the load-bearing two-call-idempotency probe.
