./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000123-20260519-104132.log:1096:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for an **APPEND-ONLY CHILD table whose ONLY public mutation surface is Append() inside a *Tx, anchored by a composite FK (organization_id, deployment_id) to a tenant-scoped parent table with ON DELETE CASCADE, whose row body has NO version column AND NO updated_at column AND NO Update/Delete surface (BEFORE UPDATE trigger rejects every UPDATE at the SQL level), AND whose tenant teardown is reachable only TRANSITIVELY via parent-deployment-cascade OR parent-organization-cascade** (BE-0460 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/deployment_event_tenant_isolation_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0458 (deployments — lifecycle-bearing table with version + updated_at trigger pair and a Cancel state-transition surface) in five load-bearing ways: (a) there is NO bump_version trigger AND NO set_updated_at trigger to anchor a cross-row UPDATE detection, because the BEFORE UPDATE trigger rejects every UPDATE outright; the bystander byte-identity assertion is therefore really proving Append's INSERT statement never even ATTEMPTS a write outside the supplied (organization_id, deployment_id) tuple. (b) The cross-tenant guard test must seed a SYMMETRIC PAIR of forged Append attempts — (orgA, bravoDep) AND (orgB, alphaDep) — and probe deployment_events by the forged ids to assert count = 0 for BOTH; a single direction would miss a regression that derived organization_id from the deployment row at write time, AND a missing forged-id-was-never-persisted probe would miss a buggy retry loop that swallowed the FK error after a phantom INSERT. (c) The cascade variants are TRANSITIVE and must be tested at BOTH depths separately — parent-deployment delete cascade (composite FK ON DELETE CASCADE on deployments(organization_id, id)) AND parent-organization delete cascade (the transitive chain organizations -> deployments -> deployment_events) — because each FK leg's organization_id scoping 