./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000123-20260519-104132.log:1097:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **TENANT-SCOPED LIFECYCLE-BEARING table whose Insert is caller-id, whose state-transition surface is a single Cancel() with optimistic-concurrency disambiguation, whose row body carries a lifecycle CHECK and a bump_version + set_updated_at trigger pair, AND whose global PRIMARY KEY (id) makes deployment ids strictly per-tenant-distinct even though every OTHER per-row anchor (UNIQUE (organization_id, idempotency_key), source_ref, source/status taxonomies) is per-tenant** (BE-0458 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/deployment_tenant_isolation_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0456 (quota_reservations — INSERT-only mutation surface, no Cancel, the row body has nullable string columns) in five load-bearing ways: (a) the bystander byte-identity assertion is anchored on TWO trigger-managed columns simultaneously — set_updated_at refreshes updated_at on any matched UPDATE AND the bump_version trigger increments version on the same UPDATE — so a regression that swept a peer tenant's row through a missing organization_id predicate surfaces as either an updated_at drift OR a version bump, not just one. (b) The Insert positive-direction pair is "same-(idempotency_key, source_ref) in two tenants both persist" instead of "same-resource in two tenants both persist", because the only per-tenant UNIQUE on this table is (organization_id, idempotency_key); the test must also assert FindByIdempotencyKey resolves the SAME key to the per-tenant row never cross-tenant — this is the read-side proof of the positive-direction shape. (c) The cross-tenant Cancel probe MUST seed BOTH a target row and an UNRELATED sibling for the same orgA, then byte-identity-assert BOTH after orgB's Cancel — without the sibling, a regression that widened the predicate by status alone (status IN ('queued','running')) would touch every queued row across both tenants and the target-only assertion would