./skills/codex/yalla-dokploy-deploy/references/rollback.md:1:# Rollback: reverting a bad deploy
./skills/codex/yalla-dokploy-deploy/references/rollback.md:6:1. **Image-tag rollback** (pre-built docker image source) — switch the `dockerImage` ref back to a previous tag, redeploy.
./skills/codex/yalla-dokploy-deploy/references/rollback.md:7:2. **Commit-rollback** (git source) — point the git source at a previous commit/tag, redeploy.
./skills/codex/yalla-dokploy-deploy/references/rollback.md:13:Rollback only makes sense when:
./skills/codex/yalla-dokploy-deploy/references/rollback.md:30:If the user invokes rollback without a recorded previous-good, ask them which version to roll back to. Inspecting `application-one` returns the current image/commit; their git log or container registry tag list gives them the previous.
./skills/codex/yalla-dokploy-deploy/references/rollback.md:32:## Image-tag rollback workflow
./skills/codex/yalla-dokploy-deploy/references/rollback.md:45:6. If the probe is healthy, swap `image` ↔ `previous_image` in .dokploy.yaml so the NEXT rollback would revert to v2.4.1 (the broken one) — UNLESS the user has tagged a different known-good
./skills/codex/yalla-dokploy-deploy/references/rollback.md:46:7. Surface a clear "rollback complete" message with the rolled-back-to version
./skills/codex/yalla-dokploy-deploy/references/rollback.md:60:## Commit-rollback workflow
./skills/codex/yalla-dokploy-deploy/references/rollback.md:73:Caveats for git rollback:
./skills/codex/yalla-dokploy-deploy/references/rollback.md:74:- Dokploy must rebuild the image from the rolled-back source. Slower than image-tag rollback (which is just a `docker pull`).
./skills/codex/yalla-dokploy-deploy/references/rollback.md:76:- Schema migrations and rollback DON'T compose cleanly: if the broken version ran an irreversible migration, rolling back the code without rolling back the DB will fail at startup. See `migrations.md` § Anti-patterns.
./skills/codex/yalla-dokploy-deploy/references/rollback.md:78:## After rollback
./skills/codex/yalla-dokploy-deploy/references/rollback.md:86:  rollback: true                         # mark this as a rollback, not a regular deploy
./skills/codex/yalla-dokploy-deploy/references/rollback.md:92:This way the **next** rollback knows to revert from v2.4.0 → v2.4.1 (the most recent prior), avoiding an undo loop where users accidentally re-roll-back to the broken version they just escaped from.
./skills/codex/yalla-dokploy-deploy/references/rollback.md:94:## When NOT to use rollback
./skills/codex/yalla-dokploy-deploy/references/rollback.md:100:- **You only need to stop traffic**: use `application-stop` (see `operations.md`), don't roll back. Rollback is for "the new version is broken AND the old version still works" — if the old version also can't run (DB schema changed), you don't have a rollback option.
./skills/codex/yalla-dokploy-deploy/references/rollback.md:106:| "Roll back the deployment" | Usually image/commit rollback — the recent code is broken |
./skills/codex/yalla-dokploy-deploy/references/rollback.md:110:| "Revert" | Could mean rollback OR a git revert that the next deploy picks up — disambiguate |
./skills/codex/yalla-dokploy-deploy/references/rollback.md:112:When the user is ambiguous, the safest read is rollback if `.dokploy.yaml.last_deploy.status` is `error` or they mention "broken / bad / hotfix / revert"; otherwise ask.
