./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000016-20260518-174147.log:584:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **project-scoped preview lifecycle table whose row points at both a preview-kind environment clone and a source environment, carries soft-delete lifecycle state, and exposes Insert + GetByID + ListByProject + Update + ScheduleDeletion + DeleteByID** (BE-0479 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/preview_environment_repository_invariants_test.go for preview_environments). The table is tenant-scoped by direct `(organization_id, project_id)` predicates and composite FKs to `environments (organization_id, project_id, id)`, uses `penv_` IDs via `domain.KindPreviewEnvironment`, and adds a trigger to enforce that the wrapped environment row has `kind='preview'` because a FK cannot express referenced-row-body predicates. It keeps status/deletion state out of customer metadata Update, and uses ScheduleDeletion (`status='deleting'`, `deletion_scheduled_at=COALESCE(..., now())`) for API teardown while preserving hard DeleteByID for worker cleanup after Dokploy teardown. Forward-applicable to future project-scoped lifecycle rows that wrap a concrete hierarchy resource with worker-owned status: test row shape/default status, deterministic `(created_at,id)` list ordering, optimistic conflicts, soft-delete lifecycle, hard-delete cleanup, constraint conflicts, referenced-row-body trigger conflicts, rollback, nil-Tx guards, and database-owned version/timestamps.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000016-20260518-174147.log:585:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for `service_backups` / PRD `backup_schedules` (BE-0477 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/service_backup_repository_invariants_test.go): this is a service-scoped desired-state leaf table with worker-projected run state. The repository surface is Insert + GetByID + ListByService + Update + MarkPending + DeleteByID; every query predicates on `(organization_id, service_id[, id])`. Insert treats `RetentionCount == 0` as omitted and normalizes it to the stable default 7, while negative/out-of-range retention still relies on the DB CHECK and maps to typed `E_CONFLICT`. Update must only write customer policy fields `(display_name, schedule, retention_count, enabled)` and preserve worker state `(status, last_run_at, last_succeeded_at)`; MarkPending is the narrow worker-signal transition and should bump version via trigger. DeleteByID is hard delete with optional If-Match, returning the deleted row snapshot. Forward-applicable to future service-scoped policy rows with worker-owned status columns: test default normalization, deterministic list ordering, optimistic conflicts, timestamp/version triggers, CHECK/FK conflicts, rollback, nil-Tx guards, and preservation of worker-owned columns during customer updates.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000016-20260518-174147.log:587:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **tenant-scoped service leaf table whose public surface is Insert + GetByID + ListByService + Update + DeleteByID, whose row has database-owned version/created_at/updated_at, and whose parent link is the composite `(organization_id, service_id)` FK** (BE-0475 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/service_domain_repository_invariants_test.go for service_domains). The reusable shape is: seed the Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service chain with existing helpers, insert through the typed repository rather than raw SQL, assert version=1 and created_at==updated_at on INSERT, assert Update bumps version and advances updated_at while preserving created_at and identity fields, assert matching If-Match succeeds while stale If-Match on Update/Delete returns typed `E_CONFLICT`, assert DeleteByID returns the final row and removes it, and cover CHECK/FK/UNIQUE violations, transaction rollback, parent cascade, deterministic ListByService ordering, and nil-Tx guards. Forward-applicable to future service-scoped leaf tables with hard-delete lifecycle and optimistic concurrency.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000016-20260518-174147.log:588:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go + PRIMARY *_test.go + INTERNAL *_internal_test.go + CANONICAL *_canonical_test.go ALL ALREADY EXIST FROM A PRIOR (NON-PRD) SUBSYSTEM STORY + VALIDATOR-EXHAUSTIVENESS STORY, AND WHOSE LIFECYCLE IS APPEND + READ + ONE STATE-TRANSITION (MarkRevoked, *Tx-bound) WITH A REPOSITORY-LEVEL LIMIT CAP, AND WHOSE UPDATE SURFACE IS GUARDED BY THREE STACKED BEFORE-UPDATE TRIGGERS (set_updated_at + bump_version + a per-column rewrite-rejecting trigger), AND WHOSE Append FILLS A ZERO StartedAt SERVER-SIDE IN THE REPOSITORY (not via DB DEFAULT)** (BE-0473 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/break_glass_repository_invariants_test.go for break_glass_sessions). Subspecies of the BE-0469 (idempotency_keys) gap-fill pattern with five load-bearing deltas: (a) the THREE-trigger UPDATE surface is unique in store — the rewrite-rejecting trigger (break_glass_sessions_reject_rewrite) raises SQLSTATE 23001 (restrict_violation) for any UPDATE that mutates a non-revocation column AND for any UPDATE that blanks the reason column. The test file walks the FULL trigger predicate matrix (id, organization_id, actor_id, actor_kind, reason, started_at, expires_at, request_id, correlation_id) via raw-SQL UPDATEs and asserts BOTH the typed error AND byte-identical row re-load AFTER the rejection. A regression that demoted any single column from the trigger predicate would surface as a per-case failure on its first iteration. The existing primary _test.go's TestBreakGlassSchemaRejectsUpdateToImmutableColumns covers ONLY reason rewrite, so the per-column-matrix invariants probe is genuinely new coverage. (b) The Append fills a zero StartedAt server-side via `if s.StartedAt.IsZero() { s.StartedAt = time.Now().UTC() }` BEFORE the INSERT — the column has NO DEFAULT in the migration (vs created_at/updated_at which DO have DEFAULT now()). A regression that removed the IsZero branch would NOT surface as a CHECK violation (expires_at > started_at is satisfied with zero started_at + any future expires_at) — it would silently store year-0001 rows. TestBreakGlassRepositoryAppendStartedAtDefaultsToNow is the regression backstop: it asserts the filled StartedAt falls within a ±2s wallclock bracket. (c) MarkRevoked's UPDATE filters on `revoked_at IS NULL` (column predicate, NOT row-existence predicate) — same shape as DriftFindingRepository.MarkResolved (BE-0471). The zero-rows branch falls through to a tenant-scoped follow-up Get inside the SAME *Tx to distinguish "missing" (typed apierr.NotFound, caller-supplied id) from "already revoked" (typed apierr.Conflict). This means TestBreakGlassRepositoryMarkRevokedCrossTenantIsNotFound is NOT redundant with TestBreakGlassRepositoryGetCrossTenantIsNotFound: a regression that dropped tenant scoping from BOTH the UPDATE and the follow-up Get would silently succeed in revoking another tenant's session. The cross-tenant Get + cross-tenant MarkRevoked pair pins both halves of the contract; either alone is insufficient. (d) The CHECK / FK violation matrix for break_glass_sessions has FOUR distinct probes that surface as typed apierr.Conflict via mapWriteError: unknown organization (FK), blank actor_kind + unknown actor_kind (both fail the CHECK actor_kind IN ('usr','sa')), blank reason (CHECK length(reason) > 0), and expires_at <= started_at (CHECK expires_at > started_at — exercised with both equal-to-started_at AND predates-started_at sub-cases). The repository does NOT pre-validate these fields (the service buildSessionToCreate is the chokepoint for validation), so the constraint violation flows through the database CHECK and surfaces via mapWriteError. (e) The `twoOrgs(t, db, f, label)` helper is genuinely new at package scope (verified via grep on `^func twoOrgs` across all store_test files — no prior declaration exists) — it names the two-organization fixture pattern (a TARGET tenant + a SUPPORT actor's home tenant) that every break_glass test needs. The 28-test shape this file lands: Append-row-shape (raw-SQL byte-equal + bgs_ prefix + server-side timestamps within ±2s + version=1 + RevokedAt nil), Append-respects-caller-supplied-id, Append-duplicate-id-Conflict (PRIMARY KEY), Append-StartedAt-defaults-to-now (the load-bearing server-side-fill probe), four CHECK/FK-violation Conflicts (unknown-org, blank-actor_kind, unknown-actor_kind, blank-reason), expires_at-not-after-started_at-Conflict (equal + predate sub-cases), Append-nil-Tx-Internal, Append-transaction-rollback, Append-peer-row-byte-identical, Get-reads-back, Get-unknown-NotFound, Get-cross-tenant-NotFound, Get-expired-returns-row-as-is (Active(t) observation, NOT Get filtering), List-newest-first-ordering (started_at DESC, id DESC), List-limit-clamping matrix (non-positive / negative / above-cap / in-range), List-unknown-org-empty-slice, MarkRevoked-happy-path (RETURNING byte-equal raw-SQL + version+1 via bump_version + updated_at advance via set_updated_at), MarkRevoked-already-revoked-Conflict (does not move revoked_at/revoked_by_*), MarkRevoked-unknown-NotFound, MarkRevoked-cross-tenant-NotFound (does not touch row), MarkRevoked-nil-Tx-Internal, MarkRevoked-peer-row-byte-identical, append-mostly-trigger-rejects-column-rewrites (per-column matrix), append-mostly-trigger-rejects-blank-reason (trigger's leading guard), organization-DELETE-cascade. New helpers introduced (per-file BreakGlass-suffix to avoid store_test collisions): rawBreakGlassRow, loadBreakGlassRowByID, countBreakGlassRowsForOrg, mintBreakGlassSessionID, breakGlassSessionFixture, seedBreakGlassSession, assertBreakGlassRowByteIdentical, breakGlassTxRollbackSentinel, sanitizeTestNameForBreakGlassID, AND the package-new twoOrgs helper for the two-org fixture shape. REUSED verbatim from sibling files: assertTimePtrEqual (drift_finding_repository_invariants_test.go), seedOrg (schema_test.go), newStore (store_test.go), wantErrCode (idempotency_test.go). NO migration added (0019_break_glass_sessions landed in an early migration). Forward-applicable to ANY future repository CRUD-and-invariants story whose table carries a three-trigger UPDATE surface (set_updated_at + bump_version + rewrite-rejecting) AND whose Append fills a zero timestamp server-side in the repository rather than via DB DEFAULT — the per-column trigger-predicate matrix and the ±2s wallclock-bracket pattern are the chokepoints that catch the two most common regression shapes (a demoted trigger column or a removed IsZero default-fill branch).
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000016-20260518-174147.log:590:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go + PRIMARY *_test.go + INTERNAL *_internal_test.go + READER ADAPTER + READER ADAPTER TEST ALL ALREADY EXIST FROM A PRIOR (NON-PRD) SUBSYSTEM STORY, AND WHOSE LIFECYCLE IS APPEND-ONLY WITH ONLY ONE WRITE SURFACE (Append) AND ONE READ SURFACE (ListByOrganization, no GetByID) AND A REPOSITORY-LEVEL LIMIT CAP, AND WHOSE INSERT COLUMN LIST DELIBERATELY OMITS occurred_at + created_at (so DEFAULT now() applies and caller-supplied timestamps are SILENTLY DISCARDED)** (BE-0467 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/audit_repository_invariants_test.go for audit_events). Subspecies of the BE-0461 (provisioning_jobs) gap-fill pattern with five load-bearing deltas: (a) the typed repository is the NARROWEST in the store package — Append + ListByOrganization only, NO GetByID, NO Update, NO Delete (row removal reachable only through organizations ON DELETE CASCADE on tenant teardown). The acceptance-criteria phrase "create typed repository methods for creating, reading, updating, and deleting ... where the lifecycle requires it" narrows to "Append + List only; the audit log lifecycle structurally rejects per-row Update / Delete" and the file header MUST document this as load-bearing so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap; (b) the audit_events INSERT column list (auditEventColumns minus occurred_at/created_at) deliberately omits both timestamp columns, so DEFAULT now() applies regardless of caller. A test `TestAuditRepositoryAppendIgnoresCallerSuppliedTimestamps` is the regression backstop — a future refactor that added occurred_at to the INSERT (so a caller could back-date an audit record) would silently break the "audit timestamps are server-side" guarantee unless this test was also updated. The probe seeds OccurredAt=year-2000 and asserts the persisted row's OccurredAt is within [now-1s, now+1s], NOT the back-dated value; (c) CHECK constraints that the application does NOT pre-validate (blank action / blank resource_kind / blank reason / actor_kind outside the closed-set ('', 'usr', 'sa', 'system')) MUST be exercised: Append does NOT pre-check these fields (the audit service is the chokepoint for the principal-mapping inputs), so the constraint violation flows through the database CHECK and surfaces as typed `apierr.Conflict` via mapWriteError. Each test asserts BOTH the error code AND `countAuditRowsForOrg(org.ID) == 0` to pin "the rejection left no row behind"; (d) the repository-level limit cap (`auditEventListMaxLimit = 200`) is a public invariant of the ListByOrganization contract and MUST be exercised in three branches — non-positive (limit <= 0 -> 200), above-cap (limit > 200 -> 200), in-range (limit in (0, 200] honoured verbatim). A regression that returned the empty slice when limit <= 0 (or every row when limit was unbounded) would surface here. The seeding helper `seedAuditRowsRawSQL` bypasses the repository and uses direct INSERT with caller-controlled occurred_at so 201 rows can be planted in O(n) without sleeping; (e) the secondary-sort ORDER BY id DESC tiebreaker on tied occurred_at is the load-bearing list-ordering invariant: a UI rendering a pair of audit events written in the same Postgres microsecond would flicker without it. The probe seeds TWO rows with the SAME occurred_at (via raw INSERT — Append's DEFAULT now() would advance between calls) and asserts listed[0].ID > listed[1].ID lexicographically. The 16-test shape this file lands: Append-row-shape (raw-SQL byte-equal + aud_ prefix + server-side timestamps within [before, after]), Append-respects-caller-supplied-id, Append-ignores-caller-supplied-timestamps (the load-bearing server-side-timestamp probe), four CHECK-violation Conflicts (blank-action, blank-resource_kind, blank-reason, unknown-actor_kind), duplicate-id-Conflict (PRIMARY KEY), Append-transaction-rollback (sentinel error + countAuditRowsForOrg == 0), Append-cascades-from-organization-delete (orgA wipe + orgB bystander byte-identity across id/organization_id/action/reason/occurred_at/created_at), Append-peer-row-byte-identical (the BE-0468 byte-identity bystander predecessor — peer Append must NOT touch a prior row), List-orders-id-DESC-on-timestamp-tie, three List-limit-clamping branches (non-positive, above-cap, in-range), List-unknown-org-returns-empty. New helpers (per-file Audit-suffix to avoid store_test collisions): rawAuditRow, loadAuditRowByID, countAuditRowsForOrg (the per-org counter — organization is the only parent FK for audit_events, so this is also the natural per-parent counter), mintAuditID, auditTxRollbackSentinel, seedAuditRowsRawSQL (the raw-INSERT batch seeder for cap-boundary tests). Reused verbatim from sibling audit_test.go: auditEventFixture, appendAudit, assertAuditEventByteIdentical; from schema_test.go: seedOrg; from store_test.go: newStore. NO migration added. Forward-applicable to any future first-typed-repository story whose underlying table is append-only with a single (Append, List) surface and a repository-level limit cap (a future security_events / system_audit table would inherit the same template, including the server-side-timestamp invariant if the INSERT column list omits the timestamp legs).
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000016-20260518-174147.log:591:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go + PRIMARY *_test.go ALREADY EXIST FROM A PRIOR (NON-PRD) SUBSYSTEM STORY, AND WHOSE LIFECYCLE HAS A CLAIM PROTOCOL (Claim + Complete + Release + Find — three writes, one read) DRIVEN BY AN UPSERT WITH AN ON-CONFLICT TARGET ON A NON-PK UNIQUE TUPLE (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key), AND WHOSE ROW BODY CARRIES A MULTI-COLUMN COMPLETION-CONSISTENT CHECK** (BE-0469 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/idempotency_repository_invariants_test.go for idempotency_keys). Subspecies of the BE-0467 (audit_events) gap-fill pattern with five load-bearing deltas: (a) the Claim INSERT uses `ON CONFLICT (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key) DO UPDATE` rather than the canonical `ON CONFLICT (id)` -- the PK is the secondary disambiguator, not the conflict target. So a duplicate id on a DIFFERENT (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key) tuple is NOT caught by ON CONFLICT; the PK violation flows through the database and surfaces through mapWriteError as apierr.Conflict. The TestIdempotencyRepositoryClaimDuplicateIDIsConflict probe MUST force the collision through caller-supplied ids on different (principal, key) tuples; a regression that switched the ON CONFLICT target to (id) would swallow the collision and silently rewrite a peer row. (b) The `idempotency_keys_completion_consistent` CHECK requires (response_status, response_body, completed_at) to be either ALL NULL (pending) or ALL NOT NULL (completed). The Complete method coerces a nil `body []byte` argument to `[]byte{}` (zero-length but non-nil) BEFORE the UPDATE so the CHECK's `response_body IS NOT NULL` clause holds for empty envelopes. TestIdempotencyRepositoryCompleteCoercesNilBodyToEmpty pins both the application-layer coercion AND the database-side non-NULL empty-bytea storage. The pending-side of the CHECK is pinned by TestIdempotencyRepositoryClaimMintsRow