./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000016-20260518-174147.log:594:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE SCHEMA ALREADY EXISTS FROM AN EARLY MIGRATION BUT WHOSE TYPED REPOSITORY IS THE FIRST STORY-LEVEL CALLER** -- i.e. the table landed in an ancient migration (dokploy_refs in 0002_tenant_hierarchy) and has been exercised only by raw-SQL schema_test.go tests; the CRUD-and-invariants PRD story creates BOTH the *_repository.go file AND the *_repository_invariants_test.go file from scratch (BE-0465 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/dokploy_ref.go + internal/controlplane/store/dokploy_ref_repository_invariants_test.go). Five load-bearing differences from a first-story-for-table CRUD vertical (BE-0457 deployments, BE-0463 job_attempts, etc.): (a) NO migration is added because the table already exists -- grep the schema_test.go assertions BEFORE writing to learn the column shape and the CHECK / UNIQUE / FK invariants the raw-SQL tests already pin; (b) the typed repository MUST NOT add columns the table lacks even if a comment in another migration suggests otherwise -- migration 0011_optimistic_versions's preamble claims to cover dokploy_refs 'for parity' but its actual ALTER statements only touch organizations / projects / environments / services, so the DokployRef struct MUST NOT carry a Version field and the repository MUST NOT include version in dokployRefColumns or scan it (the single fastest way to discover this kind of drift is the error-chain unwrap debug pattern: write a one-shot test that prints %T:%s for each `cur.(unwrapper).Unwrap()` layer, which surfaces the underlying *pgconn.PgError with the SQLSTATE 42703 'column X does not exist' message in one round trip); (c) if the table has NO customer-mutable column the typed repository surface is INSERT + GET + LIST + DELETE only -- no Update method, no optimistic-concurrency If-Match path -- and the file header MUST document this as load-bearing so a future reader does not mistake the absence for