./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000016-20260518-174147.log:598:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for an **APPEND-ONLY CHILD table whose ONLY public mutation surface is Append() inside a *Tx, anchored by a composite FK (organization_id, deployment_id) to a tenant-scoped parent table with ON DELETE CASCADE, whose row body has NO version column AND NO updated_at column AND NO Update/Delete surface (BEFORE UPDATE trigger rejects every UPDATE at the SQL level), AND whose tenant teardown is reachable only TRANSITIVELY via parent-deployment-cascade OR parent-organization-cascade** (BE-0460 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/deployment_event_tenant_isolation_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0458 (deployments — lifecycle-bearing table with version + updated_at trigger pair and a Cancel state-transition surface) in five load-bearing ways: (a) there is NO bump_version trigger AND NO set_updated_at trigger to anchor a cross-row UPDATE detection, because the BEFORE UPDATE trigger rejects every UPDATE outright; the bystander byte-identity assertion is therefore really proving Append's INSERT statement never even ATTEMPTS a write outside the supplied (organization_id, deployment_id) tuple. (b) The cross-tenant guard test must seed a SYMMETRIC PAIR of forged Append attempts — (orgA, bravoDep) AND (orgB, alphaDep) — and probe deployment_events by the forged ids to assert count = 0 for BOTH; a single direction would miss a regression that derived organization_id from the deployment row at write time, AND a missing forged-id-was-never-persisted probe would miss a buggy retry loop that swallowed the FK error after a phantom INSERT. (c) The cascade variants are TRANSITIVE and must be tested at BOTH depths separately — parent-deployment delete cascade (composite FK ON DELETE CASCADE on deployments(organization_id, id)) AND parent-organization delete cascade (the transitive chain organizations -> deployments -> deployment_events) — because each FK leg's organization_id scoping must be proven independently; a regression that dropped the organization_id leg of either FK would surface as either a bravo row vanishing or its byte content drifting. (d) The "soft-deleted rows where applicable" acceptance-criteria phrase narrows to "where applicable": deployment_events has no soft-delete column and no Delete repository surface, so cascade is the analogous proof and is the substitute test surface — the file header MUST document this delegation explicitly so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap. (e) The metadata column is jsonb stored as []byte; bystander byte-identity uses bytes.Equal not json equivalence (a regression that stripped or reordered keys would defeat json equivalence but survive bytes.Equal — and the test wants to catch ANY rewrite, semantic or representational). The 8-test shape (Append-bystander-byte-identity across queued+running+progress peers / Append-row-count-invariant / Append-foreign-parent-deployment-rejected-BOTH-directions-with-forged-id-not-persisted+bystander-byte-identity / GetByID-cross-tenant-NotFound-with-no-leak (message/metadata key/value/request_id/correlation_id/event_type/deployment_id) / ListByDeployment-cross-tenant-empty-and-counts-unchanged across per-org AND per-deployment probes / ListByDeployment-foreign-deployment-id-of-SAME-tenant-returns-empty (composite (organization_id, deployment_id) probe distinct from "foreign org+foreign deployment") / parent-deployment-delete-cascade-is-tenant-scoped across queued+running+progress orgB peers / parent-organization-delete-cascade-is-tenant-scoped) leaves BE-0459's invariants surface (CRUD/append-only-trigger/closed-set-event_type/composite-FK/duplicate-id/nil-tx/rollback/metadata-redaction/caller-supplied-occurred_at/cascade-on-deployment) untouched. Helpers reused from BE-0459: rawDeploymentEventRow, loadDeploymentEventRowByID, countDeploymentEventRowsForDeployment, mintDeploymentEventID, runAppendDeploymentEventOrFail, seedDeploymentEvent; from BE-0458: seedTwoTenantDeploymentFixture, twoTenantDeploymentFixture type; from BE-0457: seedQueuedDeployment; from store_test: seedOrg/seedProject/seedEnvironment/seedService, newStore, containsValue. New helpers introduced (unique to this file): twoTenantDeploymentEventFixture + seedTwoTenantDeploymentEventFixture (the two-tenant boilerplate that ALSO seeds one parent deployment per tenant, because events need a parent deployment as the FK target — a pure two-tenant org/proj/env/svc fixture is insufficient for events), assertDeploymentEventByteIdentical (anchored on every column INCLUDING metadata []byte via bytes.Equal; NO version/updated_at columns to anchor cross-row UPDATE detection), countDeploymentEventRowsForOrg (per-tenant aggregate variant of countDeploymentEventRowsForDeployment — the cascade tests need both, per-tenant is the cascade-scoped probe, per-deployment is the Append-scoped probe). Forward-applicable to any tenant-isolation story whose table is an append-only child anchored by a composite FK to a tenant-scoped parent with cascade (e.g. future audit_events / job_events / log lines stories).
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000016-20260518-174147.log:599:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **TENANT-SCOPED LIFECYCLE-BEARING table whose Insert is caller-id, whose state-transition surface is a single Cancel() with optimistic-concurrency disambiguation, whose row body carries a lifecycle CHECK and a bump_version + set_updated_at trigger pair, AND whose global PRIMARY KEY (id) makes deployment ids strictly per-tenant-distinct even though every OTHER per-row anchor (UNIQUE (organization_id, idempotency_key), source_ref, source/status taxonomies) is per-tenant** (BE-0458 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/deployment_tenant_isolation_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0456 (quota_reservations — INSERT-only mutation surface, no Cancel, the row body has nullable string columns) in five load-bearing ways: (a) the bystander byte-identity assertion is anchored on TWO trigger-managed columns simultaneously — set_updated_at refreshes updated_at on any matched UPDATE AND the bump_version trigger increments version on the same UPDATE — so a regression that swept a peer tenant's row through a missing organization_id predicate surfaces as either an updated_at drift OR a version bump, not just one. (b) The Insert positive-direction pair is "same-(idempotency_key, source_ref) in two tenants both persist" instead of "same-resource in two tenants both persist", because the only per-tenant UNIQUE on this table is (organization_id, idempotency_key); the test must also assert FindByIdempotencyKey resolves the SAME key to the per-tenant row never cross-tenant — this is the read-side proof of the positive-direction shape. (c) The cross-tenant Cancel probe MUST seed BOTH a target row and an UNRELATED sibling for the same orgA, then byte-identity-assert BOTH after orgB's Cancel — without the sibling, a regression that widened the predicate by status alone (status IN ('queued','running')) would touch every queued row across both tenants and the target-only assertion would miss it. (d) The not-found payload for cross-tenant GetByID must name ONLY the deployment_id the caller supplied — explicit no-leak substring assertions against the foreign source_ref / idempotency_key / service_id pin this; the apierr.NotFound("deployment", deploymentID) call site is the chokepoint. (e) The acceptance criterion "List queries return stable pagination without leaking total counts from other tenants" requires asserting BOTH the empty slice AND that the per-tenant COUNT(*) for the foreign tenant is unchanged after the read — the read must not leak into a write. The 9-test shape (Insert-bystander-byte-identity across queued+running+idem-collide peers / Insert-row-count-invariant / Insert-same-idempotency-key-in-two-tenants-both-persist with cross-FindByIdempotencyKey read-side proof / GetByID-cross-tenant-NotFound-with-no-leak / FindByIdempotencyKey-cross-tenant-returns-zero-no-leak / Cancel-cross-tenant-leaves-target+sibling-byte-identical / ListByService-cross-tenant-empty-and-counts-unchanged / ListByService-foreign-service-id-of-SAME-tenant-returns-empty (composite (organization_id, service_id) probe distinct from "foreign org+foreign svc") / organization-delete-cascade-is-tenant-scoped across queued+running+extra orgB rows) leaves BE-0457's invariants surface (CHECK/FK/duplicate/nil-tx/rollback/lifecycle disambiguation) untouched. Helpers reused from BE-0457: rawDeploymentRow, loadDeploymentRowByID, countDeploymentRowsForOrg, mintDeploymentID, runInsertDeploymentOrFail, seedQueuedDeployment, seedRunningDeployment; from store_test: seedOrg/seedProject/seedEnvironment/seedService, newStore, containsValue. New helpers introduced (unique to this file): twoTenantDeploymentFixture + seedTwoTenantDeploymentFixture (the two-tenant boilerplate is centralised because every test in this file needs both halves), assertDeploymentByteIdentical (anchored on updated_at + version simultaneously), timePtrEqualDep / fmtTimePtrDep (per-file Dep-suffix is mandatory — store_test already exposes QR, QP, and "" variants; collisions block compilation). Note that rawDeploymentRow has NO nullable string columns, so stringPtrEqualDep is deliberately not added (would be U1000 dead code). GOTCHA: when two tenants need to share a row "shape" in the cascade test, the suffixes MUST be per-tenant-unique because mintDeploymentID hashes `t.Name() + suffix` and the GLOBAL PRIMARY KEY on deployments(id) means two tenants cannot share an id even though every OTHER column is per-tenant unique — a naive same-suffix-for-both-tenants call trips mapWriteError's generic "a deployment with this idempotency key already exists" message which LOOKS like an idem-key conflict but is actually an id collision. Forward-applicable to any tenant-isolation story whose table carries a global PRIMARY KEY AND a per-tenant UNIQUE on a different column AND lifecycle triggers (e.g. future BE-0461 provisioning_jobs).
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000016-20260518-174147.log:600:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TENANT-SCOPED LIFECYCLE-BEARING table whose Insert is caller-id (no per-row id minting in the repository), whose only public state-transition surface is a single Cancel() with optimistic-concurrency disambiguation, and whose row body carries a lifecycle CHECK (terminal-status <-> finished_at) plus a bump_version trigger** (BE-0457 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/deployment_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0455 (quota_reservations — the repository mints qres_<token> ids and has no Update/Cancel surface) in three load-bearing ways: (a) the test must mint deployment ids itself (DeploymentService.Create owns domain.NewID(domain.KindDeployment); the repo does NOT mint). A test-local mintDeploymentID(t, suffix) helper using `"dep_"+t.Name()+"_"+suffix` avoids global PK collisions across parallel cases without a shared atomic counter — every *_test.go under internal/controlplane/store/ shares one package, so t.Name() is the natural disambiguator. (b) The terminal-status-on-Insert probe leverages the deployments_finished_consistent CHECK: Insert's INSERT column list omits finished_at, so any terminal Status passed in violates the CHECK by construction (status terminal AND finished_at NULL). This is the one-line proof that this surface can only mint non-terminal rows; no separate trigger test is needed. (c) Cancel exercises THREE disambiguation legs against the SAME *Tx — nil expectedVersion + terminal row -> apierr.Conflict (no current_version attached), non-nil expectedVersion + version mismatch -> apierr.ConflictStale (current_version attached, verified via apierr.CurrentVersionOf), unknown id -> apierr.NotFound. The cross-tenant Cancel probe MUST include a byte-identity assertion on the foreign-tenant row (status unchanged, finished_at still NULL, version not bumped) — without it, a regression that scoped the UPDATE by id alone and disambiguated by organization_id would silently mutate the foreign tenant's row before failing the lookup. The 31-test shape (Insert: row-shape / status-default / non-queued-status-persists / dup-id / dup-(org,idem) / blank-id / blank-requested_by / blank-idem / unknown-source / terminal-without-finished / unknown-org / cross-tenant-service / nil-tx / rollback; GetByID: persisted-row / unknown / cross-tenant; FindByIdempotencyKey: present / missing / cross-tenant; Cancel: queued / running / matching-version / stale-version-ConflictStale / terminal-row-Conflict / unknown-NotFound / cross-tenant-NotFound-with-byte-identity / nil-tx; ListByService: reverse-chronological / empty-non-nil-slice / cross-tenant-empty) leaves the BE-0458 tenant-isolation file to add the bystander byte-identical proof + organization-delete-cascade probes. Helpers reused: seedOrg/seedProject/seedEnvironment/seedService (schema_test.go), newStore (store_test.go). New helpers introduced (unique to this file): rawDeploymentRow, loadDeploymentRowByID, countDeploymentRowsForOrg, mintDeploymentID, runInsertDeploymentOrFail, seedQueuedDeployment, seedRunningDeployment, deploymentTxRollbackSentinel.
./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000016-20260518-174147.log:601:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **TENANT-SCOPED EVENT-LIKE LEDGER table whose ONLY public mutation surface is an unbounded INSERT (no UNIQUE on (organization_id, resource) — only PRIMARY KEY (id) and a composite-FK target UNIQUE (organization_id, id)), whose ONLY read aggregate is a SUM filtered by tenant + resource + status + expires_at predicates, and whose row body carries nullable timestamp/text columns** (BE-0456 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_reservation_tenant_isolation_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0454 (quota_usage — partial UNIQUE (organization_id, resource) and the ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING / SELECT FOR UPDATE primitive) in three load-bearing ways: (a) the bystander byte-identity assertion must cover the nullable job_id and settled_at columns (rawQuotaReservationRow declares them *string/*time.Time; the per-file stringPtrEqualQR/timePtrEqualQR/fmtStringPtrQR/fmtTimePtrQR helpers MUST get a per-file QR suffix because store_test already exposes QP-suffixed variants from BE-0451 and a stringPtrEqual variant from BE-0440); (b) the same-resource-in-two-tenants positive-direction pair is INSERT-and-both-persist (no conflict at all — no UNIQUE constraint to defeat) instead of LockUsage-and-both-succeed; (c) a NEW cross-tenant probe is mandatory — global PRIMARY KEY collision across tenants must surface as typed apierr.Conflict (yerr.CodeConflict) AND leave the prior owner's row byte-identical AND insert no row for the requester; this guards against a regression that swapped the INSERT for an ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE (a copy-paste of quota_policies' upsert pattern). The 7-test shape (insert-bystander-byte-identity across active+settled peers / insert-row-count-invariant / same-resource-two-tenants-both-persist / global-PK-collision-cross-tenant-conflict with byte-identical prior-owner / SumActiveReservations-tenant-scoped (10-vs-300 chosen so a cross-tenant fold shows as 310) 